WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.546 --> 00:00:02.276
- Hello, everyone.

00:00:02.276 --> 00:00:04.826
I am not Sal.

00:00:04.826 --> 00:00:07.526
Unfortunately, Sal's a little
bit under the weather today.

00:00:07.526 --> 00:00:08.746
I am Kristen DiCerbo,

00:00:08.746 --> 00:00:10.326
the chief learning
officer at Khan Academy.

00:00:10.326 --> 00:00:12.086
And I'm gonna attempt to fill

00:00:12.086 --> 00:00:13.986
a little bit of his shoes today.

00:00:13.986 --> 00:00:17.456
We are excited to have
as our homeroom guest,

00:00:17.456 --> 00:00:21.316
psychologist, New York
Times bestselling author,

00:00:21.316 --> 00:00:24.796
and monthly columnist, Lisa Damour.

00:00:24.796 --> 00:00:28.586
And she'll be talking to us
about the emotional wellbeing

00:00:28.586 --> 00:00:30.916
of our students in these COVID times.

00:00:30.916 --> 00:00:32.786
So we're looking forward to that.

00:00:32.786 --> 00:00:34.876
Before we get started,
I want to remind you

00:00:34.876 --> 00:00:38.686
that Khan Academy is a
not-for-profit organization

00:00:38.686 --> 00:00:43.686
and we do depend on donations
to keep us all going.

00:00:43.846 --> 00:00:44.806
If you would like to donate,

00:00:44.806 --> 00:00:47.886
you can go to khanacademy.org/donate.

00:00:47.886 --> 00:00:51.646
And I know that this is
a tough time for folks,

00:00:51.646 --> 00:00:55.046
but $10 even helps contribute

00:00:55.046 --> 00:00:57.386
to keeping all of these
resources up and going

00:00:57.386 --> 00:00:59.296
for folks that need it during this time.

00:00:59.296 --> 00:01:01.496
So thank you, if you can do that.

00:01:01.496 --> 00:01:04.856
I also want to recognize
some corporate folks

00:01:04.856 --> 00:01:06.016
who have stepped up,

00:01:06.016 --> 00:01:08.646
particularly in these COVID times.

00:01:08.646 --> 00:01:10.486
They've been partners with us

00:01:10.486 --> 00:01:12.786
and really helped make sure
we can keep things running.

00:01:12.786 --> 00:01:14.886
That includes google.org,

00:01:14.886 --> 00:01:18.006
AT&amp;T, Fastly, Bank of
America and Novartis.

00:01:18.006 --> 00:01:19.976
Thank you to them for
helping to contribute

00:01:19.976 --> 00:01:21.879
to keep everyone learning

00:01:21.879 --> 00:01:25.076
as we're going through these times.

00:01:25.076 --> 00:01:30.016
So with that, let me
introduce Dr. Lisa Damour.

00:01:30.016 --> 00:01:32.156
Thanks for being with us today.

00:01:32.156 --> 00:01:33.156
- Thanks for having me.

00:01:33.156 --> 00:01:34.499
I'm delighted to be here.

00:01:35.426 --> 00:01:36.259
- Excellent.

00:01:36.259 --> 00:01:40.176
So just this morning,
you published a column

00:01:40.176 --> 00:01:42.776
that speaks to teenagers with your advice

00:01:42.776 --> 00:01:45.066
about how to navigate

00:01:45.066 --> 00:01:46.736
some of this time we're living in.

00:01:46.736 --> 00:01:50.666
Can you share with us some
of the advice that you have?

00:01:50.666 --> 00:01:51.499
- Sure.

00:01:51.499 --> 00:01:53.836
So in my September column
for the New York Times,

00:01:53.836 --> 00:01:56.019
I had written a, or actually
was my August column.

00:01:56.019 --> 00:01:58.526
I had written a back to
school wellbeing checklist

00:01:58.526 --> 00:02:02.006
for parents with regard to their
adolescent's mental health.

00:02:02.006 --> 00:02:05.626
And in the comments,
several teenagers said,

00:02:05.626 --> 00:02:07.246
why don't you just write to us

00:02:07.246 --> 00:02:08.886
about what we need to
know about ourselves?

00:02:08.886 --> 00:02:12.036
And I thought as usual,
the teenagers are right.

00:02:12.036 --> 00:02:14.236
As usual, they're like
totally on the money.

00:02:15.746 --> 00:02:17.756
My editor, who's
wonderful, was in agreement

00:02:17.756 --> 00:02:19.216
that I could write this column to them.

00:02:19.216 --> 00:02:22.126
So this one is called, dear teenagers,

00:02:22.126 --> 00:02:23.196
here's what you can do to manage

00:02:23.196 --> 00:02:24.816
your own emotional wellbeing.

00:02:24.816 --> 00:02:27.506
And I talk through what
we know in psychology

00:02:27.506 --> 00:02:30.176
about how adolescents, in particular,

00:02:30.176 --> 00:02:32.606
operate when it comes to their feelings,

00:02:32.606 --> 00:02:34.796
some of the super powers they have.

00:02:34.796 --> 00:02:38.106
For example, they feel
things more intensely,

00:02:38.106 --> 00:02:42.006
which we know this means
they feel difficult

00:02:42.006 --> 00:02:43.866
and painful things more intensely.

00:02:43.866 --> 00:02:48.576
It also means they feel pleasures
and joys more intensely.

00:02:48.576 --> 00:02:51.856
And so at this time when
we are all, at this point,

00:02:51.856 --> 00:02:54.386
kind of limited to small delights,

00:02:54.386 --> 00:02:57.026
that pumpkin spice latte goes
further for a 16-year-old

00:02:57.026 --> 00:02:58.246
than it does for a 30-year-old.

00:02:58.246 --> 00:03:01.266
And that's a good thing that
they can take advantage of.

00:03:01.266 --> 00:03:03.726
And I talk with about basic
mental health maintenance

00:03:03.726 --> 00:03:05.476
and what to do when they're worried

00:03:05.476 --> 00:03:07.896
and how psychological defenses work.

00:03:07.896 --> 00:03:10.756
But it was, I will say
I've written that column

00:03:10.756 --> 00:03:11.589
for five years.

00:03:11.589 --> 00:03:12.886
I've written for the Times for seven.

00:03:12.886 --> 00:03:14.876
I think this was my favorite column

00:03:14.876 --> 00:03:16.419
I've ever gotten to write.

00:03:17.516 --> 00:03:18.446
- That is great.

00:03:18.446 --> 00:03:20.786
And I am sure that the teenagers

00:03:20.786 --> 00:03:23.416
appreciate you talking to them
because knowing teenagers,

00:03:23.416 --> 00:03:26.006
they're thinking I can do this myself.

00:03:26.006 --> 00:03:29.499
I don't need the help of my
parents to navigate this.

00:03:32.966 --> 00:03:35.473
So thinking about parents though,

00:03:36.586 --> 00:03:40.376
what kinds of things should
parents be looking out for

00:03:40.376 --> 00:03:42.896
in terms of signs that their
kids might be struggling

00:03:42.896 --> 00:03:44.096
or need some extra help?

00:03:45.376 --> 00:03:47.346
- Oof, this is such a hard time.

00:03:47.346 --> 00:03:49.976
And the baseline we're starting from

00:03:49.976 --> 00:03:52.046
is one of kids being pretty upset

00:03:52.046 --> 00:03:55.566
and pretty distressed and rightly so.

00:03:55.566 --> 00:03:57.126
We are also starting from a baseline

00:03:57.126 --> 00:04:01.206
of parents being pretty
worn thin and pretty tired

00:04:01.206 --> 00:04:04.199
and dealing with their own
stresses and anxieties.

00:04:04.199 --> 00:04:06.306
And I think in that context,

00:04:06.306 --> 00:04:08.576
it can be very hard to know
when it's time to worry.

00:04:08.576 --> 00:04:09.896
Is the kid overreacting?

00:04:09.896 --> 00:04:11.876
Is the parent overreacting?

00:04:11.876 --> 00:04:15.996
So to start at the extremes
and then to walk it back.

00:04:15.996 --> 00:04:19.876
We worry when a kid's
mood gets stuck in a rut.

00:04:19.876 --> 00:04:22.326
So we fully expect the
normal adolescent mood

00:04:22.326 --> 00:04:23.196
will go up and down.

00:04:23.196 --> 00:04:25.166
You know, that they'll
be really salty one day

00:04:25.166 --> 00:04:28.256
and then perfectly fine later
that day or the next day.

00:04:28.256 --> 00:04:30.096
That's normal development.

00:04:30.096 --> 00:04:33.776
We worry if a young person becomes down

00:04:33.776 --> 00:04:38.506
and stays down, is sad,
lethargic, unhappy, low

00:04:38.506 --> 00:04:39.986
for days at a time,

00:04:39.986 --> 00:04:43.276
that's grounds for concern, or irritable.

00:04:43.276 --> 00:04:46.686
At every opportunity, I
take it to let people know

00:04:46.686 --> 00:04:48.096
that one of the forms

00:04:48.096 --> 00:04:51.236
that depression can take in
teenagers is irritability.

00:04:51.236 --> 00:04:53.296
It's sort of unique to adolescents.

00:04:53.296 --> 00:04:56.536
And sometimes it's easy to
miss that they're depressed

00:04:56.536 --> 00:04:59.376
because an irritable teenager is,

00:04:59.376 --> 00:05:02.036
we can just dismiss it
as a snarky teenager,

00:05:02.036 --> 00:05:04.536
but any teenager who is
annoyed with their parents,

00:05:04.536 --> 00:05:06.206
annoyed with their teachers,
annoyed with their coaches,

00:05:06.206 --> 00:05:09.016
can't stand any of their
peers, that is something

00:05:09.016 --> 00:05:11.686
that should be considered
possibly depression.

00:05:11.686 --> 00:05:13.086
And we worry about anxiety.

00:05:13.086 --> 00:05:14.866
That it is too big,

00:05:14.866 --> 00:05:19.516
meaning out of proportion to
what's going on or persistent,

00:05:19.516 --> 00:05:23.076
even when things are okay or okay enough.

00:05:23.076 --> 00:05:24.686
So that's when the worry, worry.

00:05:24.686 --> 00:05:26.316
And what I would want parents to do

00:05:26.316 --> 00:05:27.966
is to reach out to a
mental health clinician

00:05:27.966 --> 00:05:30.406
or call their pediatrician even,

00:05:30.406 --> 00:05:33.899
just to check in and sort
of get a take on things.

00:05:34.836 --> 00:05:37.176
But then there's a lot below that,

00:05:37.176 --> 00:05:39.206
a lot that's less concerning than that,

00:05:39.206 --> 00:05:41.366
that's much more in the garden variety

00:05:41.366 --> 00:05:43.016
of day to day raising teenagers

00:05:43.016 --> 00:05:45.199
or pandemic raising teenagers.

00:05:46.666 --> 00:05:49.006
- Can you tell us some of the strategies

00:05:49.006 --> 00:05:51.026
that parents who have kids

00:05:51.026 --> 00:05:53.696
who haven't probably
reached that threshold yet,

00:05:53.696 --> 00:05:55.496
but are just struggling a little bit.

00:05:55.496 --> 00:05:57.836
What are some things that they can do?

00:05:57.836 --> 00:06:01.966
Well, a lot of it means just
allowing teenagers to vent.

00:06:01.966 --> 00:06:03.926
So one thing that is true about teenagers

00:06:03.926 --> 00:06:05.776
is that they complain about school.

00:06:05.776 --> 00:06:08.256
And they complained about school
all last year school year,

00:06:08.256 --> 00:06:09.416
not just this school year.

00:06:09.416 --> 00:06:12.426
It's part of how teenagers do school.

00:06:12.426 --> 00:06:15.616
And what I mean is they're
wonderful at school.

00:06:15.616 --> 00:06:18.406
Teenagers hold it together
all school day long,

00:06:18.406 --> 00:06:21.406
put up with all sorts of
hassles and annoyances,

00:06:21.406 --> 00:06:23.226
are charming through the whole thing.

00:06:23.226 --> 00:06:25.696
And part of how they do it is they collect

00:06:25.696 --> 00:06:27.336
what I call their emotional trash

00:06:27.336 --> 00:06:29.006
over the course of the day,

00:06:29.006 --> 00:06:31.606
and then they get home and
they dump it on their parents.

00:06:31.606 --> 00:06:34.976
And this is a normal and
expectable transaction.

00:06:34.976 --> 00:06:36.563
Works really well for the teenager

00:06:36.563 --> 00:06:37.796
because they tend to feel greatly relieved

00:06:37.796 --> 00:06:39.236
after this transaction.

00:06:39.236 --> 00:06:40.316
Is hard on the parents,

00:06:40.316 --> 00:06:42.711
'cause they can sometimes
feel burdened by it,

00:06:42.711 --> 00:06:46.896
but under normal conditions
and then pandemic conditions,

00:06:46.896 --> 00:06:49.606
one thing that's really
helpful for parents to say

00:06:49.606 --> 00:06:51.156
when kids are having a hard time

00:06:51.156 --> 00:06:55.436
or feeling full of complaints is to say,

00:06:55.436 --> 00:06:59.896
do you want my help with this
or do you just need to vent?

00:06:59.896 --> 00:07:03.056
And so often teenagers
and tweens will say,

00:07:03.056 --> 00:07:04.916
I just need to vent.

00:07:04.916 --> 00:07:06.726
And then the parent can just let them vent

00:07:06.726 --> 00:07:08.336
and know that they're serving basically

00:07:08.336 --> 00:07:10.686
a functional trash disposal service

00:07:10.686 --> 00:07:13.249
that lets the kiddo move forward.

00:07:14.256 --> 00:07:18.036
The trick in the pandemic
is to try to measure

00:07:18.036 --> 00:07:21.396
how much more they're complaining
this year than last year,

00:07:21.396 --> 00:07:23.056
because they are.

00:07:23.056 --> 00:07:25.716
But what I think is important
for parents to do is to think,

00:07:25.716 --> 00:07:29.176
okay, on September 29th, 2019,

00:07:29.176 --> 00:07:30.836
how much was my kid complaining?

00:07:30.836 --> 00:07:33.089
How far above that increment are we?

00:07:34.006 --> 00:07:36.296
The other thing I want
parents to be mindful of

00:07:36.296 --> 00:07:38.496
is that we're hearing
more complaints sometimes

00:07:38.496 --> 00:07:41.866
because we're standing right
there as school is happening.

00:07:41.866 --> 00:07:44.516
And so, under normal conditions,

00:07:44.516 --> 00:07:46.536
your kid has annoying
group projects that happen

00:07:46.536 --> 00:07:49.136
during the school day
that you don't hear about

00:07:49.136 --> 00:07:52.436
because by the time they get
home, they're either over it

00:07:52.436 --> 00:07:54.346
or they've complained to their friends.

00:07:54.346 --> 00:07:56.666
Under pandemic conditions,

00:07:56.666 --> 00:07:59.926
you can run into your
teenager in the kitchen

00:07:59.926 --> 00:08:01.916
when they just finished
an annoying group project,

00:08:01.916 --> 00:08:04.576
and you're gonna get the full download.

00:08:04.576 --> 00:08:08.056
So there is complaining,
there's heightened complaining,

00:08:08.056 --> 00:08:10.506
but I don't want parents to
take all of the complaining

00:08:10.506 --> 00:08:13.626
as though it's coming from a zero start.

00:08:13.626 --> 00:08:16.686
It's higher and higher
for both pandemic reasons

00:08:16.686 --> 00:08:19.009
and also just logistics of pandemics.

00:08:20.928 --> 00:08:21.896
That totally makes sense.

00:08:21.896 --> 00:08:26.106
And your comment about asking,
do you need my help or not?

00:08:26.106 --> 00:08:29.276
I think applies to emotional things,

00:08:29.276 --> 00:08:32.966
but also I know that
learning itself is hard

00:08:32.966 --> 00:08:37.026
and kids can get in a place
just in working through

00:08:37.026 --> 00:08:38.116
what they're supposed to be learning,

00:08:38.116 --> 00:08:39.696
where they're struggling.

00:08:39.696 --> 00:08:41.806
And in the educational psychology world,

00:08:41.806 --> 00:08:43.496
we talk about productive struggle

00:08:43.496 --> 00:08:46.876
and that that can actually
help people learn.

00:08:46.876 --> 00:08:48.766
But if parents are seeing that,

00:08:48.766 --> 00:08:50.226
how should they react to that?

00:08:50.226 --> 00:08:53.099
And how do they figure out
when to help and when not to?

00:08:54.106 --> 00:08:56.586
- Well for you to give
parents the gift of the idea

00:08:56.586 --> 00:09:00.226
of productive struggle
is such a good first step

00:09:00.226 --> 00:09:03.976
because as parents, we
know our kids so well,

00:09:03.976 --> 00:09:05.476
and if they are doing school

00:09:05.476 --> 00:09:07.606
like right over there next to you,

00:09:07.606 --> 00:09:10.206
you can sense, even if
they're not saying anything,

00:09:10.206 --> 00:09:12.286
you can sense when they
are starting to feel

00:09:12.286 --> 00:09:14.696
tense or frustrated.

00:09:14.696 --> 00:09:19.206
And as a parent, that makes
the parent uncomfortable.

00:09:19.206 --> 00:09:21.536
I'm a parent who has a
kid doing school at home.

00:09:21.536 --> 00:09:23.526
It makes me uncomfortable.

00:09:23.526 --> 00:09:27.086
And so then of course we
want to step in and help.

00:09:27.086 --> 00:09:28.709
Help.

00:09:28.709 --> 00:09:31.436
Sometimes we think we're
stepping in to help the kid.

00:09:31.436 --> 00:09:34.156
I will confess that I have
sometimes felt compelled to do it

00:09:34.156 --> 00:09:35.666
because it's bothering me.

00:09:35.666 --> 00:09:37.286
Like, I want to make it stop

00:09:37.286 --> 00:09:39.936
so that I can focus on
what I need to focus on.

00:09:39.936 --> 00:09:43.866
And my kid's discomfort
is a distraction for me,

00:09:43.866 --> 00:09:44.836
so I want to make a stop.

00:09:44.836 --> 00:09:46.746
Not because I think my kiddo needs it,

00:09:46.746 --> 00:09:50.216
but because I sort of selfishly want it.

00:09:50.216 --> 00:09:52.476
So the first step is to recognize

00:09:52.476 --> 00:09:55.186
that learning is grappling,

00:09:55.186 --> 00:10:00.186
and that kids getting
frustrated and struggling along

00:10:00.366 --> 00:10:02.226
is often when learning happens.

00:10:02.226 --> 00:10:04.506
And like you mentioned, teachers know this

00:10:04.506 --> 00:10:07.846
and teachers develop the
capacity to tolerate,

00:10:07.846 --> 00:10:11.926
and in fact, encourage
struggle in the classroom.

00:10:11.926 --> 00:10:13.966
And so that idea of taking the guidance

00:10:13.966 --> 00:10:15.166
around emotional wellbeing

00:10:15.166 --> 00:10:17.596
and transferring it over
to academic wellbeing

00:10:17.596 --> 00:10:19.616
to say, give parents a language,

00:10:19.616 --> 00:10:21.316
to say something like,

00:10:21.316 --> 00:10:24.226
are you doing okay with that struggle?

00:10:24.226 --> 00:10:26.106
Do you need any support
around that struggle?

00:10:26.106 --> 00:10:29.352
But not even to try to
get rid of the struggle,

00:10:29.352 --> 00:10:31.406
or mostly just to bite our tongues

00:10:31.406 --> 00:10:33.876
until a kiddo maybe sends up a flag.

00:10:33.876 --> 00:10:34.709
But that's hard.

00:10:34.709 --> 00:10:36.689
It's really easy to say, hard to do.

00:10:37.686 --> 00:10:38.956
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:10:38.956 --> 00:10:40.326
And I know it's hard too

00:10:40.326 --> 00:10:43.656
if you're a parent who's
trying to work over here

00:10:43.656 --> 00:10:46.729
and your kid's on the computer over here.

00:10:48.476 --> 00:10:50.956
Having them know when
they can ask questions

00:10:50.956 --> 00:10:54.116
and when it's okay to kind of jump in

00:10:54.116 --> 00:10:56.366
as opposed to when you're
in the middle of doing

00:10:56.366 --> 00:10:58.849
homeroom session, or whatever that may be.

00:10:59.736 --> 00:11:02.186
I think it's another piece
that I've talked about

00:11:02.186 --> 00:11:05.226
can be helpful for parents as well,

00:11:05.226 --> 00:11:07.106
to kind of give their kids a plan

00:11:07.106 --> 00:11:09.876
for what to do when they need help,

00:11:09.876 --> 00:11:11.956
and so they know what to do.

00:11:11.956 --> 00:11:14.246
- Absolutely.
- We are getting some.

00:11:14.246 --> 00:11:15.296
Go ahead.

00:11:15.296 --> 00:11:19.216
- I've sometimes encouraged
families under normal conditions

00:11:19.216 --> 00:11:21.536
to actually have a list
of things kids can do

00:11:21.536 --> 00:11:22.906
when they feel stuck.

00:11:22.906 --> 00:11:24.826
Because for me, the big
challenges in school

00:11:24.826 --> 00:11:27.466
are getting started and
managing when stuck.

00:11:27.466 --> 00:11:29.306
And so it's really helpful to,

00:11:29.306 --> 00:11:31.646
I've had kids put a Post-it
note next to their homework

00:11:31.646 --> 00:11:33.726
of all of the options
for when they get stuck.

00:11:33.726 --> 00:11:35.426
Move on to something else,

00:11:35.426 --> 00:11:37.866
ask the teacher for more
instruction, phone a friend.

00:11:37.866 --> 00:11:40.586
I mean, there's lots of
things that kids can do

00:11:40.586 --> 00:11:43.436
and we can articulate those,
give kids lots of options,

00:11:43.436 --> 00:11:45.956
brainstorm options when
they're not doing their work.

00:11:45.956 --> 00:11:48.546
So that the first outlet isn't necessarily

00:11:48.546 --> 00:11:51.246
the parent rescuing or the
kid asking for parent help.

00:11:52.836 --> 00:11:54.316
- Fantastic.

00:11:54.316 --> 00:11:56.666
So we are getting some good questions in

00:11:56.666 --> 00:11:58.556
from Facebook and YouTube.

00:11:58.556 --> 00:11:59.576
Let me take one of those.

00:11:59.576 --> 00:12:02.626
From Facebook, Rosa Ballenger asks,

00:12:02.626 --> 00:12:04.316
how do you get your kid motivated

00:12:04.316 --> 00:12:06.316
when they're not turning in assignments?

00:12:06.316 --> 00:12:08.736
What's the best approach to handle that?

00:12:08.736 --> 00:12:10.576
Okay, so there's two pieces in there.

00:12:10.576 --> 00:12:11.656
One is motivation.

00:12:11.656 --> 00:12:13.026
The other is not turning in assignments.

00:12:13.026 --> 00:12:15.136
Those are two different topics.

00:12:15.136 --> 00:12:17.536
Let's start with the not
turning in assignments.

00:12:20.136 --> 00:12:23.816
I think one of the real
challenges in parenting

00:12:23.816 --> 00:12:25.686
is to tolerate kids having

00:12:25.686 --> 00:12:27.916
to sort it out with their teachers.

00:12:27.916 --> 00:12:32.056
And in my experience, if
parents don't rescue kids

00:12:32.056 --> 00:12:34.336
and kids have to face the
music with their teachers,

00:12:34.336 --> 00:12:35.356
and I trust teachers.

00:12:35.356 --> 00:12:37.026
By and large they handle this really well

00:12:37.026 --> 00:12:38.936
and really effectively.

00:12:38.936 --> 00:12:41.466
Usually kids don't continue
to make that mistake

00:12:41.466 --> 00:12:44.236
if they've had to really
have it out with the teacher.

00:12:44.236 --> 00:12:46.216
And so if the parent can't stand it,

00:12:46.216 --> 00:12:48.016
they might email the teacher and say,

00:12:48.016 --> 00:12:50.786
I am well aware that
she's behind on her work.

00:12:50.786 --> 00:12:52.276
I'm gonna leave it to you to figure out

00:12:52.276 --> 00:12:53.526
what you think needs to be done here

00:12:53.526 --> 00:12:56.586
or to give her the
consequences that is coming.

00:12:56.586 --> 00:12:59.086
One thing when I have gotten
to be with groups of kids

00:12:59.086 --> 00:13:03.266
in person is I'll sometimes
have them write notes of things

00:13:03.266 --> 00:13:04.506
they want me to tell their parents.

00:13:04.506 --> 00:13:05.626
So I visited schools and I'm like,

00:13:05.626 --> 00:13:07.456
what do you want your parents to know?

00:13:07.456 --> 00:13:11.246
And I am blown away by how
often I've had young people

00:13:11.246 --> 00:13:13.666
write me a note saying, please tell them

00:13:13.666 --> 00:13:17.046
to just let me deal with the
consequences of my choices.

00:13:17.046 --> 00:13:18.906
Don't try to intervene.

00:13:18.906 --> 00:13:22.586
And so kids can do that
and benefit from that.

00:13:22.586 --> 00:13:25.796
Okay, then there's the
motivation question,

00:13:25.796 --> 00:13:27.756
which for me right now in the pandemic

00:13:27.756 --> 00:13:30.266
is like the million dollar question

00:13:30.266 --> 00:13:32.186
because motivation is low.

00:13:32.186 --> 00:13:34.866
And it's not that motivation
is high all the time

00:13:34.866 --> 00:13:36.486
under normal conditions.

00:13:36.486 --> 00:13:38.196
It's that it's lower now

00:13:38.196 --> 00:13:41.236
because kids are not caught
up in the current of school.

00:13:41.236 --> 00:13:43.156
They're not alongside
their friends doing work

00:13:43.156 --> 00:13:45.086
they might not really feel like doing.

00:13:45.086 --> 00:13:47.356
They're not being pushed
along by encouragement

00:13:47.356 --> 00:13:48.736
in person from their teachers

00:13:48.736 --> 00:13:50.756
in the same ways they might be used to.

00:13:50.756 --> 00:13:54.146
So the challenges of motivation
that are always present

00:13:54.146 --> 00:13:56.106
in education are now laid,

00:13:56.106 --> 00:13:59.156
I think, more bare by the pandemic.

00:13:59.156 --> 00:14:02.376
And I think that it lets
us have a conversation

00:14:02.376 --> 00:14:07.036
that's probably long overdue
about motivation in school.

00:14:07.036 --> 00:14:08.546
And the short on that conversation,

00:14:08.546 --> 00:14:10.466
and then Kristen, I'll let you ask me

00:14:10.466 --> 00:14:12.303
which direction you want to go with this.

00:14:12.303 --> 00:14:16.236
The short on that is that I don't think

00:14:16.236 --> 00:14:19.686
we're really honest
with kids about the fact

00:14:19.686 --> 00:14:21.766
that a lot of really
high-functioning students

00:14:21.766 --> 00:14:23.766
and high-functioning adults

00:14:23.766 --> 00:14:27.476
sometimes need rewards to get work done.

00:14:27.476 --> 00:14:29.226
I think we've sort of made it seem like

00:14:29.226 --> 00:14:30.296
you shouldn't need that

00:14:30.296 --> 00:14:33.286
or only sort of the lesser students

00:14:33.286 --> 00:14:35.359
require that kind of support.

00:14:37.216 --> 00:14:38.049
- Absolutely.

00:14:38.049 --> 00:14:41.356
I know for myself that sometimes

00:14:41.356 --> 00:14:45.516
when I have to write a
piece, write an article,

00:14:45.516 --> 00:14:48.406
I say, okay, if I write this page,

00:14:48.406 --> 00:14:50.526
I can have this Hershey's
Kiss as a reward.

00:14:50.526 --> 00:14:54.146
So how do you think about,
what are appropriate rewards

00:14:54.146 --> 00:14:57.759
and how can we best use
them to help kids succeed?

00:14:59.296 --> 00:15:02.326
- Well, let's take a zoom
out and then zoom back in.

00:15:02.326 --> 00:15:05.826
So the language we tend to
use in education for rewards

00:15:05.826 --> 00:15:08.456
is extrinsic motivation,
that you're doing it

00:15:08.456 --> 00:15:10.246
because somebody's giving you something.

00:15:10.246 --> 00:15:12.406
It's a grade or money or chocolate,

00:15:12.406 --> 00:15:13.916
I'm right with you on the chocolate.

00:15:13.916 --> 00:15:14.946
For me, it's coffee.

00:15:14.946 --> 00:15:17.606
For me, it is getting to take

00:15:17.606 --> 00:15:19.556
a lap around the block, walking.

00:15:19.556 --> 00:15:21.376
I have a long list of rewards

00:15:21.376 --> 00:15:24.406
that I give myself to do work at times.

00:15:24.406 --> 00:15:26.126
So we call those extrinsic motivators

00:15:26.126 --> 00:15:28.446
and they're in contrast
to intrinsic motivators,

00:15:28.446 --> 00:15:31.176
which is basically, the kid
does it 'cause they love it.

00:15:31.176 --> 00:15:32.686
Like, they just are totally fascinated

00:15:32.686 --> 00:15:34.296
and drawn to the work.

00:15:34.296 --> 00:15:37.476
So we have generally and understandably

00:15:38.726 --> 00:15:40.646
put intrinsic motivation on a pedestal.

00:15:40.646 --> 00:15:43.106
Like that's the ideal, that's
what we're working toward.

00:15:43.106 --> 00:15:45.896
And I'm all for that idea.

00:15:45.896 --> 00:15:49.046
I think all students should
come to all of their work

00:15:49.046 --> 00:15:52.006
open to the idea that they
might find it fascinating.

00:15:52.006 --> 00:15:54.426
I think all educators
should do what they can

00:15:54.426 --> 00:15:57.016
to present work in a way that makes it

00:15:57.016 --> 00:16:00.276
as fascinating as possible to
as many students as possible.

00:16:00.276 --> 00:16:02.696
So when you have intrinsic
motivation on your side,

00:16:02.696 --> 00:16:04.346
what you have is effortless attention.

00:16:04.346 --> 00:16:07.026
There's nothing to it,
and that's fantastic.

00:16:07.026 --> 00:16:08.396
It doesn't always happen.

00:16:08.396 --> 00:16:11.816
So then you still need
a gear you can slip into

00:16:11.816 --> 00:16:13.166
to get it done.

00:16:13.166 --> 00:16:15.396
That's the extrinsic motivation gear.

00:16:15.396 --> 00:16:18.326
There need not be any shame about having

00:16:18.326 --> 00:16:19.406
to sometimes use that gear.

00:16:19.406 --> 00:16:20.239
You use it.

00:16:20.239 --> 00:16:21.072
I use it.

00:16:21.072 --> 00:16:21.905
We love our work.

00:16:21.905 --> 00:16:23.026
We're doing our favorite subject.

00:16:23.026 --> 00:16:26.026
We're getting paid and
we still need that gear.

00:16:26.026 --> 00:16:29.416
And so the first step I think
is to be honest with kids,

00:16:29.416 --> 00:16:31.146
to say, that's okay that
you're not into the work.

00:16:31.146 --> 00:16:32.606
It still needs to get done.

00:16:32.606 --> 00:16:33.656
What's the play?

00:16:33.656 --> 00:16:35.376
How are we gonna do it?

00:16:35.376 --> 00:16:39.056
And I have been blown
away by the creativity

00:16:39.056 --> 00:16:40.396
students are sharing with me

00:16:40.396 --> 00:16:42.516
about how they're getting work done.

00:16:42.516 --> 00:16:45.216
Students have been telling
me about YouTube study videos

00:16:45.216 --> 00:16:46.986
that they pull up where
you can go on YouTube

00:16:46.986 --> 00:16:50.326
and find videos of very studious

00:16:50.326 --> 00:16:53.056
and thoughtful students, it seems,

00:16:53.056 --> 00:16:55.126
working in libraries and
they turn on the video

00:16:55.126 --> 00:16:58.396
and they work alongside
the person in the video.

00:16:58.396 --> 00:17:00.116
I had a fabulous student tell me

00:17:00.116 --> 00:17:03.436
that she makes time-lapse
videos of herself studying

00:17:03.436 --> 00:17:06.496
and she does it to keep herself focused

00:17:06.496 --> 00:17:08.456
because she wants to have
a good time-lapse video.

00:17:08.456 --> 00:17:12.066
So she'll work at a 25
to 50 minute unit of work

00:17:12.066 --> 00:17:14.446
uninterrupted so that she
could have a really funny

00:17:14.446 --> 00:17:16.936
time-lapse video or a very
complete time-lapse video

00:17:16.936 --> 00:17:18.406
to show her parents or her friends.

00:17:18.406 --> 00:17:19.706
And she said, they're very funny.

00:17:19.706 --> 00:17:21.166
I love the faces I make
when concentrating.

00:17:21.166 --> 00:17:23.586
- Oh my gosh.
- Brilliant, brilliant.

00:17:23.586 --> 00:17:24.426
So there's that.

00:17:24.426 --> 00:17:25.259
There's also chocolate.

00:17:25.259 --> 00:17:26.276
There's also getting to roll around

00:17:26.276 --> 00:17:27.256
on the floor with the dog.

00:17:27.256 --> 00:17:28.706
There's getting to take
a lap around the block

00:17:28.706 --> 00:17:29.796
with a parent.

00:17:29.796 --> 00:17:32.896
There is social media.

00:17:32.896 --> 00:17:34.606
Work really hard for 25 minutes,

00:17:34.606 --> 00:17:36.386
take five minutes to check everybody out

00:17:36.386 --> 00:17:38.599
on whatever social
media platform you want.

00:17:39.536 --> 00:17:42.976
It doesn't matter to me so
much what the reward is.

00:17:42.976 --> 00:17:44.696
I think it's great when kids are creative.

00:17:44.696 --> 00:17:46.426
I think it's great when
we make space for them

00:17:46.426 --> 00:17:48.536
to come up with things we
would have never thought of.

00:17:48.536 --> 00:17:52.016
What matters to me is we
remove the shame from it,

00:17:52.016 --> 00:17:54.836
that there's no reason to feel embarrassed

00:17:54.836 --> 00:17:55.996
about the gimmicks or hacks

00:17:55.996 --> 00:17:57.286
if you need them to get the work done.

00:17:57.286 --> 00:17:59.069
The work has to get done.

00:18:00.916 --> 00:18:01.836
- That's fantastic.

00:18:01.836 --> 00:18:05.306
And I love working with your kids

00:18:05.306 --> 00:18:08.736
to decide what's rewarding to them.

00:18:08.736 --> 00:18:10.506
So that's something that they value

00:18:10.506 --> 00:18:11.826
and is important for them.

00:18:11.826 --> 00:18:13.776
But yeah, that's fantastic.

00:18:13.776 --> 00:18:17.256
I had heard of people doing
Zoom calls with each other

00:18:17.256 --> 00:18:21.506
and just turning the
video on to work together

00:18:21.506 --> 00:18:24.546
as they're doing, as a
way to just keep connected

00:18:24.546 --> 00:18:26.496
with each other, so all kinds of ideas.

00:18:27.458 --> 00:18:28.646
- Absolutely, or just if you're lonely.

00:18:28.646 --> 00:18:30.486
So another option is
for the parent to say,

00:18:30.486 --> 00:18:32.546
do you want me to come work alongside you?

00:18:32.546 --> 00:18:34.006
I can clear my email all day long.

00:18:34.006 --> 00:18:35.509
I can clear it next to you.

00:18:36.616 --> 00:18:37.916
When I was in graduate school,

00:18:37.916 --> 00:18:41.186
I mean, I have great discipline
and I love my subject

00:18:41.186 --> 00:18:42.019
and I love what I do.

00:18:42.019 --> 00:18:45.416
And I am like the queen
of hacks to get work done.

00:18:45.416 --> 00:18:46.756
And when I was in graduate school,

00:18:46.756 --> 00:18:49.786
my best friend and I used
to sit across a table

00:18:49.786 --> 00:18:51.226
from each other at a coffee shop

00:18:51.226 --> 00:18:53.696
when we had 40 undergraduate
papers to grade,

00:18:53.696 --> 00:18:55.086
and we played this game

00:18:55.086 --> 00:18:57.186
where we could only speak to each other

00:18:57.186 --> 00:18:59.076
after we'd each graded five papers.

00:18:59.076 --> 00:19:01.696
And so we would plow
through five, talk for 10,

00:19:01.696 --> 00:19:02.776
plow through five.

00:19:02.776 --> 00:19:05.926
We got done in probably four hours

00:19:05.926 --> 00:19:09.226
what if I had been left to my
own devices in my apartment

00:19:09.226 --> 00:19:11.226
would have taken me to the whole weekend.

00:19:11.226 --> 00:19:13.646
And those are the conversations

00:19:13.646 --> 00:19:16.886
that grownups are not having with kids

00:19:16.886 --> 00:19:21.126
and that we owe kids, and
they're left with this sense of,

00:19:21.126 --> 00:19:23.306
oh, the good students feel like doing it

00:19:23.306 --> 00:19:26.036
or some day my motivation will show up.

00:19:26.036 --> 00:19:27.806
I'm stuck till then.

00:19:27.806 --> 00:19:30.959
That is not a helpful
way to try to do school.

00:19:32.686 --> 00:19:33.676
- Absolutely.

00:19:33.676 --> 00:19:34.509
Fantastic.

00:19:34.509 --> 00:19:36.496
Let's take another question from Facebook.

00:19:36.496 --> 00:19:38.596
We have a Maya Aplig.

00:19:38.596 --> 00:19:40.976
I'm gonna kill this last name, Giock.

00:19:40.976 --> 00:19:43.446
And again, apologize for that last name.

00:19:43.446 --> 00:19:48.446
She asks, I have a 13-year-old
son that is screen-obsessed.

00:19:48.456 --> 00:19:50.966
I have struggled to
set healthy boundaries.

00:19:50.966 --> 00:19:52.096
He's seeing a psychologist,

00:19:52.096 --> 00:19:54.076
but I would love to hear
your advice might be

00:19:54.076 --> 00:19:57.236
for setting healthy
screen-time boundaries.

00:19:57.236 --> 00:20:02.136
- Okay, so these are my
rules and they are pandemic

00:20:02.136 --> 00:20:03.936
and also pre and post-pandemic.

00:20:03.936 --> 00:20:06.399
These are my general rules
for kids and screens.

00:20:07.246 --> 00:20:10.406
First rule, what really
matters first and foremost

00:20:10.406 --> 00:20:12.426
in my mind is what's on the screen.

00:20:12.426 --> 00:20:14.466
What screens deliver is such a wide range.

00:20:14.466 --> 00:20:15.616
So school is fabulous.

00:20:15.616 --> 00:20:17.186
Good for kids, fantastic.

00:20:17.186 --> 00:20:20.076
Educational TV, all for it.

00:20:20.076 --> 00:20:21.136
Banal TV.

00:20:21.136 --> 00:20:22.546
If kids need a break and just a time

00:20:22.546 --> 00:20:23.946
to sort of relax and rest.

00:20:23.946 --> 00:20:25.676
Happy socializing with their friends.

00:20:25.676 --> 00:20:26.606
Kids need to socialize.

00:20:26.606 --> 00:20:27.886
Screens are where that's at right now.

00:20:27.886 --> 00:20:31.089
So there's all sorts of good
stuff that happens on screens.

00:20:31.936 --> 00:20:34.716
I'm okay with hours of
good stuff on screens.

00:20:34.716 --> 00:20:36.736
That's what a lot of kids
are doing these days.

00:20:36.736 --> 00:20:39.266
I'm not okay with five
seconds of porn, right?

00:20:39.266 --> 00:20:41.086
Or super violent, yucky stuff.

00:20:41.086 --> 00:20:44.786
So first and foremost was on
the screens can't be yucky.

00:20:44.786 --> 00:20:47.203
Can't be something that
kids should not be seeing,

00:20:47.203 --> 00:20:49.756
and that doesn't get articulated enough.

00:20:49.756 --> 00:20:51.556
So that's the first guardrail.

00:20:51.556 --> 00:20:55.686
The second guardrail is that
screens cannot get in the way

00:20:55.686 --> 00:20:58.766
of what is necessary for
healthy development in children.

00:20:58.766 --> 00:20:59.926
Okay, so here's my list.

00:20:59.926 --> 00:21:00.856
This is my short list.

00:21:00.856 --> 00:21:02.616
We can add to this list.

00:21:02.616 --> 00:21:04.856
First and foremost, sleep.

00:21:04.856 --> 00:21:06.436
Sleep, sleep, sleep.

00:21:06.436 --> 00:21:09.106
Elementary school kids
need 11 hours a night.

00:21:09.106 --> 00:21:11.726
Middle schoolers need 10 hours a night.

00:21:11.726 --> 00:21:14.496
High schoolers need nine hours a night.

00:21:14.496 --> 00:21:16.416
This is actually happening
more than it usually does

00:21:16.416 --> 00:21:17.696
because kids aren't commuting

00:21:17.696 --> 00:21:20.336
or unfortunately doing afterschool stuff,

00:21:20.336 --> 00:21:23.296
but sleep and screens are mortal enemies.

00:21:23.296 --> 00:21:25.266
I don't think screens
should be in bedrooms.

00:21:25.266 --> 00:21:27.816
I totally get it, that some
families don't have the space

00:21:27.816 --> 00:21:29.026
to do otherwise.

00:21:29.026 --> 00:21:31.896
I totally get it, that some
kids are having to do school

00:21:31.896 --> 00:21:33.036
in their bedrooms.

00:21:33.036 --> 00:21:34.366
Understand completely.

00:21:34.366 --> 00:21:35.766
It should come out at night.

00:21:35.766 --> 00:21:37.306
So sleep.

00:21:37.306 --> 00:21:38.206
Physical activity.

00:21:38.206 --> 00:21:39.896
Kids should be up and moving around.

00:21:39.896 --> 00:21:41.496
We have to work harder
to make that happen.

00:21:41.496 --> 00:21:43.086
It's worth it.

00:21:43.086 --> 00:21:44.906
Face-to-face interactions.

00:21:44.906 --> 00:21:47.046
Reading people's faces,
picking up social cues.

00:21:47.046 --> 00:21:49.256
Screens should not get in the way of that.

00:21:49.256 --> 00:21:52.396
Screens should not get in
the way of focused work.

00:21:52.396 --> 00:21:54.936
Kids should not have screens
interrupting their ability

00:21:54.936 --> 00:21:56.346
to read with focus,

00:21:56.346 --> 00:21:58.346
even do one thing on a screen with focus.

00:21:58.346 --> 00:22:00.536
Sometimes kids are doing like schoolwork

00:22:00.536 --> 00:22:02.016
and then popping over to their phone

00:22:02.016 --> 00:22:04.746
to check their social media, not okay.

00:22:04.746 --> 00:22:06.666
And screens should not get in the way

00:22:06.666 --> 00:22:09.086
of being helpful around the house,

00:22:09.086 --> 00:22:11.396
around the community, all of the above.

00:22:11.396 --> 00:22:14.916
So my advice to parents is
decide what matters to you

00:22:14.916 --> 00:22:17.536
and then protect it from screens.

00:22:17.536 --> 00:22:22.186
And be for those things,
not against screens.

00:22:22.186 --> 00:22:24.896
And then the goal of course
is to have so much activity

00:22:24.896 --> 00:22:26.896
and so much time helping
out around the house

00:22:26.896 --> 00:22:28.586
and so much excellent sleep

00:22:28.586 --> 00:22:32.946
that the time left for
screens is minimized.

00:22:32.946 --> 00:22:35.476
But then the time on
screens is spent doing stuff

00:22:35.476 --> 00:22:37.046
that actually makes kids' lives better

00:22:37.046 --> 00:22:38.266
or gives them some relaxation.

00:22:38.266 --> 00:22:39.266
Doesn't do any harm.

00:22:41.366 --> 00:22:44.566
- That's sounds very
actionable and useful.

00:22:44.566 --> 00:22:45.786
Thank you.

00:22:45.786 --> 00:22:48.876
I have another question coming
in about prioritization,

00:22:48.876 --> 00:22:50.306
like in that way.

00:22:50.306 --> 00:22:52.636
So from Facebook, Catherine Terry says,

00:22:52.636 --> 00:22:55.106
my son is really struggling to engage

00:22:55.106 --> 00:22:57.176
with virtual school and get his work done.

00:22:57.176 --> 00:23:00.366
I know he's emotionally
exhausted, as we all are.

00:23:00.366 --> 00:23:03.146
I feel like beating up
on him about academics

00:23:03.146 --> 00:23:05.236
is not as important as
helping him navigate

00:23:05.236 --> 00:23:06.626
and take care of his mental health

00:23:06.626 --> 00:23:08.476
and would love to know your thoughts.

00:23:09.356 --> 00:23:11.276
- I agree completely.

00:23:11.276 --> 00:23:14.306
My mantra for the pandemic is this.

00:23:14.306 --> 00:23:16.666
The goal is actually not to see

00:23:16.666 --> 00:23:19.766
how productive and
disciplined everybody can be.

00:23:19.766 --> 00:23:20.896
The goal is for all of us

00:23:20.896 --> 00:23:24.476
to get to the other side
of this emotionally intact.

00:23:24.476 --> 00:23:27.206
So there may be moments
where you feel like

00:23:27.206 --> 00:23:30.716
I'm gonna choose my kid's
wellbeing over the perfection,

00:23:30.716 --> 00:23:33.776
or even maybe the completion
of that assignment.

00:23:33.776 --> 00:23:36.656
I think that is a fair call right now.

00:23:36.656 --> 00:23:40.436
The other thing though
that's a good half step is

00:23:40.436 --> 00:23:42.326
to just go ahead and empathize with him

00:23:42.326 --> 00:23:46.216
about how hard the school piece is.

00:23:46.216 --> 00:23:48.726
I think sometimes when parents
feel like they can't fix it,

00:23:48.726 --> 00:23:50.816
they feel like they're helpless.

00:23:50.816 --> 00:23:52.086
You're not.

00:23:52.086 --> 00:23:54.776
There is a space between
fixing and doing nothing.

00:23:54.776 --> 00:23:57.396
And that is the empathy
place where we just say,

00:23:57.396 --> 00:23:58.336
buddy, I get it.

00:23:58.336 --> 00:24:02.766
I cannot imagine how hard it
must be to do school this way.

00:24:02.766 --> 00:24:05.186
It's like, school is all
vegetables, no dessert.

00:24:05.186 --> 00:24:06.576
This is no fun.

00:24:06.576 --> 00:24:07.906
I totally get it.

00:24:07.906 --> 00:24:09.566
That will help your son out.

00:24:09.566 --> 00:24:13.146
The other thing that I think
we cannot overdo right now

00:24:13.146 --> 00:24:16.286
is just admiring how
well our kids are doing

00:24:16.286 --> 00:24:18.386
in spite of what's being asked.

00:24:18.386 --> 00:24:21.126
I think parents should be
saying constantly to their kids,

00:24:21.126 --> 00:24:23.066
you are doing a really good job with this.

00:24:23.066 --> 00:24:24.906
I see how hard you are trying.

00:24:24.906 --> 00:24:27.536
I am so impressed with how much attention

00:24:27.536 --> 00:24:31.006
you're giving this, given
how kind of dry or distant

00:24:31.006 --> 00:24:33.146
or disconnected it feels.

00:24:33.146 --> 00:24:33.979
I see it.

00:24:33.979 --> 00:24:35.146
I'm impressed.

00:24:35.146 --> 00:24:35.996
Way to go.

00:24:35.996 --> 00:24:39.329
I mean, there is no
limit on that right now.

00:24:40.896 --> 00:24:41.876
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:24:41.876 --> 00:24:43.776
And when we talked about motivation,

00:24:43.776 --> 00:24:45.736
we know that if you feel
like you're failing,

00:24:45.736 --> 00:24:47.776
you're gonna be even less
motivated to do something.

00:24:47.776 --> 00:24:49.616
And if you feel like
you're having some success,

00:24:49.616 --> 00:24:52.976
even if it's small successes,
that gives you more motivation

00:24:52.976 --> 00:24:55.376
to keep trying and keep
doing what you're doing.

00:24:55.376 --> 00:24:57.746
So I think that positive reinforcement

00:24:57.746 --> 00:24:58.979
helps communicate that.

00:25:01.846 --> 00:25:05.896
All right, I've got another question

00:25:05.896 --> 00:25:08.296
that I know everyone is asking,

00:25:08.296 --> 00:25:11.776
thinking about how kids are struggling

00:25:11.776 --> 00:25:14.046
because they are lacking
now the social interaction

00:25:14.046 --> 00:25:15.366
that they usually have.

00:25:15.366 --> 00:25:19.516
Do you have any suggestions
about how we can bring

00:25:19.516 --> 00:25:21.306
some of that experience to kids?

00:25:21.306 --> 00:25:23.856
How we can help them feel
connected to their peers?

00:25:24.896 --> 00:25:28.716
- Well, most kids, a lot of kids

00:25:28.716 --> 00:25:31.266
are using social media to stay connected.

00:25:31.266 --> 00:25:33.466
It's interesting how quickly
we've sort of decided

00:25:33.466 --> 00:25:34.646
we're okay with social media,

00:25:34.646 --> 00:25:37.289
which I actually, I am on that train.

00:25:38.546 --> 00:25:40.306
Kids should see their friends.

00:25:40.306 --> 00:25:42.346
They should get to socialize in person.

00:25:42.346 --> 00:25:45.436
What this means is that parents
probably have to supervise.

00:25:45.436 --> 00:25:47.976
And that's the challenge
because parents' plates

00:25:47.976 --> 00:25:49.726
are already so full.

00:25:49.726 --> 00:25:52.276
They're trying to run homes,

00:25:52.276 --> 00:25:54.566
do jobs outside the home sometimes,

00:25:54.566 --> 00:25:56.906
and also sort of monitor school.

00:25:56.906 --> 00:25:58.606
Parents are down to their last minute.

00:25:58.606 --> 00:26:00.246
Parents were spread very thin.

00:26:00.246 --> 00:26:05.246
And the idea of now having to
make some sort of organized

00:26:05.376 --> 00:26:07.856
socializing happen where
an adult can be available

00:26:07.856 --> 00:26:10.356
is just one more layer on top of that.

00:26:10.356 --> 00:26:13.056
And yet kids do need to see
their friends in person.

00:26:13.056 --> 00:26:15.016
What I would say to
parents to make it easier,

00:26:15.016 --> 00:26:16.676
see if you can make a routine.

00:26:16.676 --> 00:26:18.556
Have a standing play date with a kid

00:26:18.556 --> 00:26:19.726
that your kid gets along with.

00:26:19.726 --> 00:26:22.106
Maybe trade off supervision
duties with that parent,

00:26:22.106 --> 00:26:24.426
but don't go week to week
trying to figure this out,

00:26:24.426 --> 00:26:27.036
'cause that just takes too much energy.

00:26:27.036 --> 00:26:29.596
But what I would say, and
this is important and helpful

00:26:29.596 --> 00:26:33.256
for kids to hear, when psychologists say

00:26:33.256 --> 00:26:35.076
everybody needs lots of social support,

00:26:35.076 --> 00:26:36.996
which we do right now,

00:26:36.996 --> 00:26:39.836
I think people often hear everybody needs

00:26:39.836 --> 00:26:43.636
a big and busy social life,
which is not what we mean.

00:26:43.636 --> 00:26:45.586
We actually mean something quite specific

00:26:45.586 --> 00:26:47.066
in terms of social support.

00:26:47.066 --> 00:26:50.606
And this is true at all
ages, for kids and parents.

00:26:50.606 --> 00:26:54.326
We mean everybody needs somebody
to tell their worries to.

00:26:54.326 --> 00:26:57.946
Everybody needs somebody they
can tell their secrets to.

00:26:57.946 --> 00:27:00.806
And everybody needs a person or a group

00:27:00.806 --> 00:27:03.826
who helps them to feel
connected and accepted.

00:27:03.826 --> 00:27:06.666
So some adults get this
entirely from their partner.

00:27:06.666 --> 00:27:09.416
Some kids get this from one best friend.

00:27:09.416 --> 00:27:11.036
Some kids are getting the worries piece

00:27:11.036 --> 00:27:12.616
covered by telling their siblings,

00:27:12.616 --> 00:27:15.076
the secrets thing covered
by telling their friends

00:27:15.076 --> 00:27:16.586
they go to camp with out of state,

00:27:16.586 --> 00:27:19.356
and the connected and accepted
thing, one good friend,

00:27:19.356 --> 00:27:20.996
or they actually really like their folks,

00:27:20.996 --> 00:27:23.506
that they've got those bases covered.

00:27:23.506 --> 00:27:26.586
So when I'm talking with
kids about their worries

00:27:26.586 --> 00:27:28.576
about their social lives right now,

00:27:28.576 --> 00:27:30.366
I like to run down that list,

00:27:30.366 --> 00:27:33.446
worries, secrets, connected and accepted.

00:27:33.446 --> 00:27:36.026
See if they are checking
all of those boxes.

00:27:36.026 --> 00:27:39.436
If they're not, that's
something we want to get on.

00:27:39.436 --> 00:27:43.946
If they are, I then say
to them, that's your cake.

00:27:43.946 --> 00:27:45.066
That's what you really need.

00:27:45.066 --> 00:27:47.026
You've got those bases covered.

00:27:47.026 --> 00:27:49.466
Everything else is icing.

00:27:49.466 --> 00:27:52.826
And it's useful both in truly evaluating

00:27:52.826 --> 00:27:54.026
the scale of the problem,

00:27:54.026 --> 00:27:56.266
which is often smaller than it looked.

00:27:56.266 --> 00:27:58.736
And also in terms of reassuring kids

00:27:58.736 --> 00:28:00.196
that they're doing it right.

00:28:00.196 --> 00:28:02.106
Because of course they're
looking on social media

00:28:02.106 --> 00:28:04.006
or they're aware, it
feels like everybody else

00:28:04.006 --> 00:28:05.676
is hanging out all the time

00:28:05.676 --> 00:28:09.626
and it's greatly reassuring to
them to get concrete guidance

00:28:09.626 --> 00:28:11.426
around what's really necessary here.

00:28:13.996 --> 00:28:17.366
- Your guidance has been
so helpful and useful.

00:28:17.366 --> 00:28:19.626
For folks that want to hear more of this,

00:28:19.626 --> 00:28:22.206
I know you have a book out in 2020.

00:28:22.206 --> 00:28:24.026
Can you tell us a little bit about that

00:28:24.026 --> 00:28:27.666
and what folks can expect from that?

00:28:27.666 --> 00:28:29.236
So I have two New York Times bestsellers,

00:28:29.236 --> 00:28:31.469
one published in 2016 and one 2019.

00:28:32.538 --> 00:28:35.296
The one from 2016 is Untangled,

00:28:35.296 --> 00:28:37.726
which is about normal
development in adolescent girls,

00:28:37.726 --> 00:28:40.196
though 80% of it applies to boys.

00:28:40.196 --> 00:28:44.076
The one from 2019 is about the epidemic

00:28:44.076 --> 00:28:45.566
of stress and anxiety in girls,

00:28:45.566 --> 00:28:47.886
which seems like way too timely now.

00:28:47.886 --> 00:28:51.436
Again, 80% of that applies to boys.

00:28:51.436 --> 00:28:53.946
But I also just launched a podcast

00:28:53.946 --> 00:28:55.866
with my colleague Reenan Ninan

00:28:55.866 --> 00:28:59.336
and it's called Ask Lisa:
The Psychology of Parenting.

00:28:59.336 --> 00:29:00.896
It comes out every Tuesday morning.

00:29:00.896 --> 00:29:02.556
It's 25 minutes long.

00:29:02.556 --> 00:29:05.656
And every week we take a
question from a parent.

00:29:05.656 --> 00:29:07.086
You can also send questions to me.

00:29:07.086 --> 00:29:08.896
If you go to my website,
there's information

00:29:08.896 --> 00:29:10.616
about how to send in questions

00:29:10.616 --> 00:29:15.616
and we tackle whatever is top
of mind for parents right now,

00:29:15.646 --> 00:29:18.946
because what we're finding is
the pandemic moves so quickly.

00:29:18.946 --> 00:29:22.946
The pressures on parents change so rapidly

00:29:22.946 --> 00:29:26.046
that once a week lets
us really being nimble

00:29:26.046 --> 00:29:28.486
in response to what is weighing on parents

00:29:28.486 --> 00:29:29.889
and hard at home right now.

00:29:32.366 --> 00:29:33.336
- That sounds great.

00:29:33.336 --> 00:29:36.176
Thank you so much for offering
all this support to parents

00:29:36.176 --> 00:29:37.496
as we all try to get through this.

00:29:37.496 --> 00:29:40.849
We really appreciate your
coming to join us today.

00:29:41.706 --> 00:29:44.766
So I will, from here, just
close and let folks know

00:29:44.766 --> 00:29:46.846
that if they want more discussion

00:29:46.846 --> 00:29:48.276
about motivation topics,

00:29:48.276 --> 00:29:52.806
tomorrow we have a
webinar that is happening

00:29:52.806 --> 00:29:55.836
at 4:00 p.m. Pacific, at 7:00 Eastern,

00:29:55.836 --> 00:29:58.466
and there's a link there that
you can sign up for that.

00:29:58.466 --> 00:30:01.806
And of course we will
have homeroom tomorrow.

00:30:01.806 --> 00:30:04.966
Hopefully Sal will have
recovered and can join us.

00:30:04.966 --> 00:30:08.286
Our guest tomorrow is doctor

00:30:08.286 --> 00:30:10.916
or Superintendent Austin Buetner

00:30:10.916 --> 00:30:13.416
from the Los Angeles
Unified School District.

00:30:13.416 --> 00:30:15.876
And so we'll be talking about
some of the challenges faced

00:30:15.876 --> 00:30:17.866
from the district perspective.

00:30:17.866 --> 00:30:20.589
Thank you all for joining
us and have a good day.

